<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Everything else in perch's Info.plist is generated (GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE
  is still YES; Xcode merges its keys into this file). This partial exists for
  one key that has no INFOPLIST_KEY_ build setting: NSServices.

  This is perch's ONLY Finder door, and it is the one that works. Perch used to
  ship a second — a `PerchFinderAction` Action Extension — on the belief that
  the two landed in different submenus. MEASURED on macOS 26 (field test
  2026-08-23), they did not: both drew under "Services", the menu read
  "Add to Perch Shelf (Perch.app)" twice, the extension never rendered under
  "Quick Actions" despite being listed and enabled there, and clicking its row
  shelved nothing — its App Group mailbox was never written. The extension was
  removed on that product call — the reading could not discriminate on a Mac
  carrying 40 registered copies. Clean LaunchServices first: docs/feel-testing.md.

  So this entry is NOT at the context menu's top level beside "New Terminal Tab
  Here" — no classic Service is, on macOS 26. It is under Services, and that is
  where the Settings pane tells people to look.

  The menu title is $(PERCH_SERVICE_TITLE), a project-level build setting, and
  the two configurations deliberately disagree: Release says "Add to Perch
  Shelf", Debug says "Add to Perch Shelf (Debug)". macOS lists one Services row
  per registered bundle id, and every xcodebuild here registers the app it built
  and nothing ever unregisters it — so a Mac that has run a dev build carries
  two providers of the same command. Both bundles are named Perch.app, so macOS
  disambiguated them identically ("Add to Perch Shelf (Perch.app)" twice) and
  the second row read as the extension bug above coming back. MEASURED
  2026-08-25: it wasn't — the second provider was com.hausfold.perch.dev, the
  `bench try` dev app in ~/.cache/bench/perch-dd, still registered from an
  August feel-test. `bench try` builds -configuration Debug (see the workshop's
  `bench`), which is why Debug is the half that gets the suffix: the shipped,
  notarized Release app is the only one that can claim the plain title. Note
  the suffix follows the configuration, not the id — a lane's plain Debug build
  is `com.hausfold.perch` and wears it too, so the release id's single row can
  read "(Debug)" until that build is unregistered (`lsregister -u <path>`,
  measured 2026-08-25: the row then falls through to the next copy of the id).

  NSRequiredContext is deliberately empty — every restricting key you add is
  another reason for macOS to hide the item, and the send types below are
  already the filter.

  Those send types are wider than Finder on purpose. Keeping the text and URL
  types means selected text or a link in Mail, Safari or an editor offers the
  same command from that app's Services menu, and it lands on the shelf as the
  same staged text a drag would make. Narrowing this to file URLs would buy a
  tidier menu at the cost of the one place a Service beats a drag: sending a
  selection from an app you cannot drag out of.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSServices</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSMenuItem</key>
			<dict>
				<key>default</key>
				<string>$(PERCH_SERVICE_TITLE)</string>
			</dict>
			<key>NSMessage</key>
			<string>addToShelf</string>
			<key>NSPortName</key>
			<string>Perch</string>
			<key>NSRequiredContext</key>
			<dict/>
			<key>NSSendTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSFilenamesPboardType</string>
				<string>public.file-url</string>
				<string>public.url</string>
				<string>public.plain-text</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
